.TH FORK 2
.SH NAME
fork  \-  spawn new process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.B pid_t fork(void);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fork
is the only way new processes are created.
The new process's core image is a copy of
that of the caller of
.IR fork .
The only distinction
is the fact
that the value returned in the old (parent) process
contains the process ID
of the new (child) process,
while the value returned in the child is 0.
Process ID's range from 1 to 30,000.
This process ID is used by
.IR wait (2).
.PP
.B Note:
that is the value C sees.  At the gate,
.B each side gets the OTHER side's pid in the accumulator
\- distinct, but not self-identifying \- and it is the
.I second
result, in r12, that says which side you are: 1 in the child, 0 in the parent.
The stub sorts it out.  That is why this stub is hand-written rather than
generated; see
.IR intro (2).
.PP
Files open before the fork
are shared, and have a common read-write pointer.
In particular,
this is the way that standard input and output
files are passed and also how
pipes are set up.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
wait(2), exec(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Returns \-1 and fails to create a process if:
there is inadequate swap space,
the user is not super-user and has too many processes,
or the system's process table is full.
Only the super-user can take the last process-table slot.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_fork
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
